\title{The Memory Knee of an Attention Profile:\\ Grids, Dilution, and Collision Mass}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{23 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Limited-memory inference for autoregressive attention models retains only the $k$ heaviest keys at each step, where $k$ is chosen so that the retained attention mass clears a fixed bar $\tau$. The minimal such $k$ --- the \emph{knee} of the attention profile --- is estimated in practice by sweeping an arithmetic budget grid such as $\{8,16,24,32\}$. A measurement on French prose in which \emph{no} grid point reached the bar (retention $0.9648$ at budget $24$ for context $512$; $0.9680$ at budget $32$ for context $1024$), despite full-context accuracy on French ($0.584$, $0.591$) exceeding that on English prose ($0.446$, $0.461$), motivates the theory developed here.

Working with an abstract attention profile $p:\NN\to\RR_{\ge0}$, its prefix mass $M_p(k)=\sum_{i<k}p(i)$, and its knee $K_p(\tau)=\min\{k:\tau\le M_p(k)\}$, we prove: (i) a failed probe certifies only $K_p(\tau)>g$, and the excess is provably invisible to the grid; (ii) mass-preserving $r$-fold token dilution obeys the sharp multiplicative sandwich $r(K-1)<K_{\mathrm{dil}}\le rK$, so no additive domain-shift law can hold; (iii) with variable tokens-per-word $w$ the diluted knee is bracketed by cumulative token counts of the top attended words, $C_w(K-1)<K_{\mathrm{dil}}\le C_w(K)$; (iv) the collision (R\'enyi-2) mass gives a tokenizer-free bound $K_p(\tau)\ge\tau^2/C$, which is attained and which scales by the same factor $r$ under dilution; (v) geometric budget grids bracket the knee within a factor $2$ at logarithmic cost, whereas arithmetic grids supply no upper bound, and mixed traffic must be provisioned by the maximum of component knees. Full-context accuracy and the knee are shown to be logically independent.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} attention profile; memory knee; prefix mass; token dilution; tokens-per-word; collision mass; R\'enyi-2 entropy; geometric grid; budget sweep.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The engineering setting}

Autoregressive attention models maintain a cache of key--value pairs for every token processed. Its size grows linearly in context length and it occupies the scarcest memory on the accelerator. \emph{Limited-memory} (top-$k$ retention) inference discards all but the $k$ heaviest keys, relying on the empirical observation that attention distributions are concentrated: a small prefix of the sorted attention weights carries almost all the mass.

Choosing $k$ is a measurement problem. One fixes a \emph{retention bar} $\tau$ and seeks the smallest budget clearing it. Because each evaluation is expensive, one evaluates at a few budgets: a \emph{grid sweep}, usually arithmetic.

\subsection{The motivating measurement}

A characterization campaign had established knees inside such grids for English prose, source code, and mathematical text, with domain-to-domain differences of about $\pm4$ keys --- one fine step of the grid --- suggesting an additive ``domain shift'' rule. A subsequent cell moved to French prose:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lccl}
\toprule
context & best grid point & retained mass & verdict\\
\midrule
$512$ & $24$ & $0.9648$ & bar not met; knee $>24$\\
$1024$ & $32$ & $0.9680$ & bar not met; knee $>32$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Full-context accuracy on French was $0.584$ and $0.591$, \emph{higher} than English prose's $0.446$ and $0.461$. Every pre-registered additive bracket was refuted: the shift exceeded $+8$ keys, escaping the grid entirely.

Two questions follow. What does a failed sweep license one to conclude? And what functional form does a domain shift take? This paper answers both and derives the corresponding experimental-design correction.

\section{The model}

\begin{definition}[Attention profile]
An \emph{attention profile} is a function $p:\NN\to\RR$ with $p(i)\ge0$ for all $i$; it is \emph{sorted} when antitone, $i\le j\Rightarrow p(j)\le p(i)$. The value $p(i)$ is the attention mass carried by the $i$-th heaviest key.
\end{definition}

Sortedness is a modelling convenience; almost all results below need only nonnegativity.

\begin{definition}[Retained mass]
For $k\in\NN$, the \emph{retained mass} at budget $k$ is $M_p(k)=\sum_{i<k}p(i)$.
\end{definition}

Immediately: $M_p(k+1)=M_p(k)+p(k)$; if $p\ge0$ then $M_p$ is nondecreasing; and $M_{p+q}=M_p+M_q$, $M_{cp}=c\,M_p$.

\begin{definition}[Knee]
The \emph{knee} of $p$ at bar $\tau$ is $K_p(\tau)=\inf\{k\in\NN:\tau\le M_p(k)\}$, the least budget meeting the bar.
\end{definition}

Three properties are used throughout:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(K\arabic*)]
\item if $\tau\le M_p(k)$ then $K_p(\tau)\le k$;
\item if the bar is attainable then $\tau\le M_p(K_p(\tau))$;
\item if $k<K_p(\tau)$ then $M_p(k)<\tau$.
\end{enumerate}
Together they characterize the knee: if $\tau\le M_p(\kappa)$ and $M_p(j)<\tau$ for all $j<\kappa$, then $K_p(\tau)=\kappa$.

\paragraph{Calibration objects.} The \emph{flat profile} $u_n(i)=\mathbf 1[i<n]$ has $M_{u_n}(k)=\min(k,n)$. The \emph{scaled flat profile} $s_{c,n}=c\,u_n$ has $M_{s_{c,n}}(k)=c\min(k,n)$. The \emph{two-level profile} $T_{g,N,c}=u_g+c(u_N-u_g)$, for $g\le N$ and $0\le c\le1$, is nonnegative and antitone with
\[
M_{T_{g,N,c}}(k)=\min(k,g)+c\bigl(\min(k,N)-\min(k,g)\bigr).
\]

\section{What a grid sweep certifies}

\begin{theorem}[Grid lower bound]\label{thm:gate}
Let $p\ge0$, let the bar be attainable, and suppose a probe fails: $M_p(g)<\tau$. Then $g<K_p(\tau)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $K_p(\tau)\le g$, then by (K2) and monotonicity $\tau\le M_p(K_p(\tau))\le M_p(g)<\tau$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
If $G$ is a nonempty finite grid all of whose points fail the bar, then $\max G<K_p(\tau)$. In particular, for the grid $\{8,16,24,32\}$ with all four probes below the bar, $K_p(\tau)\ge33$.
\end{corollary}

This is the entire content of the French measurement at context $1024$. Nothing about the magnitude of the excess is licensed --- and this is not conservatism.

\begin{theorem}[Grid underdetermination]\label{thm:under}
Let $g<N$ and $\tau=g+1$. There is a nonnegative antitone profile $p$ with $M_p(k)=k$ for every $k\le g$ (hence $M_p(k)<g+1$ there), $M_p(k)=g+1$ for every $k\ge N$, and $K_p(g+1)=N$ exactly.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $p=T_{g,N,c}$ with $c=1/(N-g)\in(0,1]$. For $k\le g$, $M_p(k)=k<g+1$. For $g\le j<N$, $M_p(j)=g+(j-g)/(N-g)<g+1$, so no budget below $N$ meets the bar; and $M_p(N)=g+c(N-g)=g+1$ does. The characterization gives $K_p(g+1)=N$, and $M_p(k)=g+1$ for $k\ge N$.
\end{proof}

Since retention is pinned at \emph{every} budget up to the ceiling, these profiles --- one for each $N>g$ --- are indistinguishable to any sweep confined to $\{0,\dots,g\}$, while their knees range over $\{g+1,g+2,\dots\}$.

\begin{corollary}[No upper bound from an arithmetic grid]\label{cor:noupper}
For every ceiling $g$ and every $B$ there are nonnegative antitone profiles $p,q$ with $M_p(k)=M_q(k)$ for all $k\le g$, $K_p(g+1)=g+1$, and $K_q(g+1)>B$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Gap ambiguity]\label{thm:gap}
Let $a<b$. There exist nonnegative antitone $p,q$ with $M_p(k)=M_q(k)$ for all $k\le a$ and all $k\ge b$, while $K_p(a+1)=a+1$ and $K_q(a+1)=b$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $p=u_{a+1}$ and $q=T_{a,b,1/(b-a)}$ as in Theorem~\ref{thm:under} with $g=a,N=b$. Below $a$ both retain $k$; above $b$ both retain $a+1$; the knees are $a+1$ and $b$.
\end{proof}

Hence a grid with consecutive probes $a<b$ cannot certify a bracket tighter than the \emph{ratio} $b/(a+1)$: ratio, not difference, is the natural resolution parameter.

\section{Uniform token dilution and the multiplicative law}

\begin{definition}[Uniform token dilution]
For $r\ge1$, the $r$-fold dilution of $p$ is $(D_rp)(j)=p(\lfloor j/r\rfloor)/r$: each semantic unit $i$ is spelt with $r$ tokens, each carrying an equal share $p(i)/r$ of the unit's mass.
\end{definition}

Dilution preserves nonnegativity and antitonicity (constant within a word, nonincreasing across words).

\begin{lemma}[Mass preservation on whole words]\label{lem:block}
For $r\ge1$ and $m\in\NN$, $M_{D_rp}(rm)=M_p(m)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $m$: the block $[rm,rm+r)$ contributes $r$ copies of $p(m)/r$, i.e.\ exactly $p(m)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Partial words]
For $0\le s\le r$, $M_{D_rp}(rm+s)=M_p(m)+s\,p(m)/r$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Dilution law]\label{thm:dilution}
Let $r\ge1$, $p\ge0$, the bar attainable, and $K=K_p(\tau)>0$. Then
\[
r(K-1)\;<\;K_{D_rp}(\tau)\;\le\;rK.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Upper: $M_{D_rp}(rK)=M_p(K)\ge\tau$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:block} and (K2); apply (K1). Lower: $M_{D_rp}(r(K-1))=M_p(K-1)<\tau$ by (K3); the bar is attainable for $D_rp$ (witness $rK$), so Theorem~\ref{thm:gate} gives $r(K-1)<K_{D_rp}(\tau)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Both ends attained]\label{thm:sharp}
For $r\ge1$ and $m\le n$, with $p=u_n$ and $\tau=m$ one has $K_p(\tau)=m$ and $K_{D_rp}(\tau)=rm$; and a flat profile with a slightly lowered bar attains $K_{D_rp}(\tau)=r(K-1)+1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:block}, $D_ru_n=s_{1/r,\,rn}$, whose knee at bar $\tau$ is the least $\kappa$ with $\kappa/r\ge\tau$, namely $\lceil r\tau\rceil$. Take $\tau=m$ for the upper end and a bar just above $K-1$ for the lower.
\end{proof}

Thus the sandwich is optimal as a function of $r$ and $K$ alone.

\begin{theorem}[No additive domain-shift law]\label{thm:noadd}
For every $d\in\NN$ there are a nonnegative antitone profile $p$, an attainable bar $\tau$, and $r\ge1$ with $K_p(\tau)+d<K_{D_rp}(\tau)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $p=u_{d+2}$, $\tau=d+2$, $r=d+2$. Then $K_p(\tau)=d+2$ and, by Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}, $K_{D_rp}(\tau)=(d+2)^2>(d+2)+d$ for all $d\ge0$.
\end{proof}

This formally refutes the ``$\pm4$ fine-step'' rule: it is not a poor fit but the wrong functional form.

\begin{theorem}[Dilution semigroup]
$D_r\circ D_s=D_{rs}$ for all $r,s\ge1$; consequently $K_{D_{rs}p}(\tau)\le r\,(s\,K_p(\tau))$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$(D_r(D_sp))(j)=p(\lfloor\lfloor j/r\rfloor/s\rfloor)/(rs)=p(\lfloor j/(rs)\rfloor)/(rs)$. The knee bound follows by applying Theorem~\ref{thm:dilution} twice.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Budget conversion]
For $r\ge1$, $p\ge0$ and any budget $g$, $M_{D_rp}(g)\le M_p(\lfloor g/r\rfloor+1)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$g\le r(\lfloor g/r\rfloor+1)$; apply monotonicity and Lemma~\ref{lem:block}.
\end{proof}

Operationally: dilution converts a \emph{token} budget into a \emph{word} budget of about $g/r$. A grid measured in tokens shrinks, semantically, by the factor $r$.

\section{Variable tokenization: the exact mechanism}

Let $w:\NN\to\NN$ with $w(i)\ge1$ be the tokens-per-word profile and $C_w(m)=\sum_{i<m}w(i)$ its cumulative count; $C_w$ is monotone and $m\le C_w(m)$.

\begin{definition}[Variable dilution]
Word $i$ occupies the token block $[C_w(i),C_w(i+1))$. With $\omega(j)=\min\{m:j<C_w(m+1)\}$ the word containing token $j$, set $(V_wp)(j)=p(\omega(j))/w(\omega(j))$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:varblock}
$M_{V_wp}(C_w(m))=M_p(m)$ for all $m$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The block $[C_w(m),C_w(m)+w(m))$ consists of the $w(m)$ tokens with $\omega=m$, each of mass $p(m)/w(m)$, summing to $p(m)$; induct on $m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Variable dilution law]\label{thm:vardil}
Let $w\ge1$ pointwise, $p\ge0$, the bar attainable, and $K=K_p(\tau)>0$. Then
\[
C_w(K-1)\;<\;K_{V_wp}(\tau)\;\le\;C_w(K).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:varblock}, $M_{V_wp}(C_w(K))=M_p(K)\ge\tau$, giving the upper bound by (K1); and $M_{V_wp}(C_w(K-1))=M_p(K-1)<\tau$ by (K3), so Theorem~\ref{thm:gate} gives the strict lower bound.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[The sharp prediction]
The diluted knee equals, up to the width of one word, \emph{the number of tokens the tokenizer spends on the top-$K$ attended words}. The correct predictor is therefore $\overline{w}_{\mathrm{top}\text{-}K}=C_w(K)/K$, not the corpus average; the two diverge exactly when attention concentrates on words of atypical token cost, as with accented and elided high-frequency French function words.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}[Ratio band]\label{cor:band}
If $L\le w(i)\le R$ for all $i$, then $L(K-1)<K_{V_wp}(\tau)\le RK$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
$Lm\le C_w(m)\le Rm$ by induction; combine with Theorem~\ref{thm:vardil}.
\end{proof}

Two languages with sufficiently separated ratio bands therefore have knees whole grid ranges apart. For constant $w\equiv r$ one has $V_wp=D_rp$, recovering Theorem~\ref{thm:dilution}.

\section{Collision mass: a tokenizer-free lower bound}

\begin{definition}[Collision mass]
The \emph{collision mass} (R\'enyi-2 mass) of the top $k$ keys is $C_p(k)=\sum_{i<k}p(i)^2$.
\end{definition}

$C_p$ is monotone in $k$. Small collision mass means flat, high-entropy attention; large means peaked.

\begin{theorem}[Cauchy--Schwarz for attention budgets]\label{thm:cs}
For all $p$ and $k$, $M_p(k)^2\le k\cdot C_p(k)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Cauchy--Schwarz on $\{0,\dots,k-1\}$ with the constant vector $1$ and $p$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Entropy bound on the knee]\label{thm:entropy}
Let $\tau>0$, $C>0$, suppose $C_p(k)\le C$ for all $k$, and let the bar be attainable. Then $\tau^2/C\le K_p(\tau)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $\kappa=K_p(\tau)$. By (K2), $\tau\le M_p(\kappa)$, so $\tau^2\le M_p(\kappa)^2\le\kappa\,C_p(\kappa)\le\kappa C$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:cs}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness]\label{thm:sharpC}
For $n\ge1$ and the flat probability profile $s_{1/n,n}$: $C(k)\le1/n$ for all $k$, the knee at bar $\tau=1$ is exactly $n$, and $\tau^2/C=n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$C_{s_{c,n}}(k)=c^2\min(k,n)$, so with $c=1/n$ this is at most $1/n$. Retention is $\min(k,n)/n$, first reaching $1$ at $k=n$.
\end{proof}

Equality holds, so no function of the collision mass yields a better bound than $\tau^2/C$.

\begin{theorem}[Dilution divides collision mass exactly]\label{thm:Cdil}
For $r\ge1$ and all $m$, $C_{D_rp}(rm)=C_p(m)/r$. Consequently, if $C_p\le C$ uniformly then $C_{D_rp}\le C/r$ uniformly.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The block $[rm,rm+r)$ contributes $r$ copies of $(p(m)/r)^2$, i.e.\ $p(m)^2/r$; induct. For the consequence, given $k$ set $m=\lfloor k/r\rfloor+1\ge k/r$, so by monotonicity $C_{D_rp}(k)\le C_{D_rp}(rm)=C_p(m)/r\le C/r$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The entropy route reproduces the multiplicative law]\label{thm:amplify}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:entropy}, for $r\ge1$ and the bar attainable for $D_rp$,
\[
r\cdot\frac{\tau^2}{C}\;\le\;K_{D_rp}(\tau).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:Cdil}, $C/r$ is a uniform collision bound for $D_rp$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:entropy} and rewrite $\tau^2/(C/r)=r\tau^2/C$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:dilution} obtains the factor $r$ from a combinatorial fact about block splitting; Theorem~\ref{thm:amplify} obtains the same $r$ from Cauchy--Schwarz and the exact scaling of the R\'enyi-2 mass. The two derivations share no lemmas beyond the definition of the knee. This upgrades the mechanism hypothesis into a prediction measurable from attention maps alone: the French cell should exhibit a \emph{smaller} collision mass than the English cell at the same context, with the collision-mass ratio tracking the knee ratio.
\end{remark}

\section{Design consequences}

\begin{theorem}[Geometric grid brackets the knee]\label{thm:geo}
Let $p\ge0$ and let the bar be attainable. Let $S=\min\{i:\tau\le M_p(2^i)\}$, well defined since $k\le2^k$. Then $K_p(\tau)\le2^S$, and if $S>0$ then $2^S<2K_p(\tau)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The first claim is (K1) applied to the witness $2^S$. For the second, minimality of $S$ gives $M_p(2^{S-1})<\tau$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:gate} yields $2^{S-1}<K_p(\tau)$; double both sides and use $2\cdot2^{S-1}=2^S$.
\end{proof}

A geometric sweep therefore returns a two-sided bracket of ratio $2$ with $\lceil\log_2K\rceil+1$ probes, and a multiplicative tax of factor $r$ only shifts $S$ by $\log_2r$: it translates along the instrument rather than escaping it. By Corollary~\ref{cor:noupper} an arithmetic grid supplies no upper bound at all, and by Theorem~\ref{thm:gap} no grid can beat the ratio of consecutive probes --- a bound the geometric grid attains with ratio $2$.

\begin{proposition}
$M_{sp+(1-s)q}(k)=s\,M_p(k)+(1-s)\,M_q(k)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Mixture law]\label{thm:mix}
Let $p,q\ge0$, $0\le s\le1$, and let the bar be attainable for both. Then
\[
\min\bigl(K_p(\tau),K_q(\tau)\bigr)\;\le\;K_{sp+(1-s)q}(\tau)\;\le\;\max\bigl(K_p(\tau),K_q(\tau)\bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
At budget $\max(K_p,K_q)$ both components meet the bar, hence so does any convex combination; apply (K1). At any budget below $\min(K_p,K_q)$ both are strictly below the bar by (K3), hence so is the combination.
\end{proof}

Because Theorem~\ref{thm:noadd} forbids interpolating budgets between domains, Theorem~\ref{thm:mix} is the correct provisioning rule for mixed multilingual traffic: budget by the maximum, never by an average.

\begin{definition}[Domain cell]
A \emph{domain cell} is a pair consisting of a full-context accuracy $a\in\RR$ and a sorted attention profile $p\ge0$; its knee at $\tau$ is $K_p(\tau)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Accuracy/knee decoupling, both signs]\label{thm:decouple}
There exist four domain cells $D_1,D_2,D_3,D_4$ taking only two accuracy values, $a(D_1)=a(D_3)<a(D_2)=a(D_4)$, with $K_{D_1}(1)<K_{D_2}(1)$ and $K_{D_4}(1)<K_{D_3}(1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $A=s_{1,4}$, so $K_A(1)=1$, and $B=s_{1/2,4}$, so $K_B(1)=2$. Put $D_1=(0,A)$, $D_2=(1,B)$, $D_3=(0,B)$, $D_4=(1,A)$. Then $K_{D_1}=1<2=K_{D_2}$ (higher accuracy, larger knee) and $K_{D_4}=1<2=K_{D_3}$ (higher accuracy, smaller knee).
\end{proof}

Hence no function --- in particular no monotone one --- maps full-context accuracy to the memory knee: accuracy measures predictability of the text, the knee measures concentration of the attention.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Geometric knee bracketing.} Given a retention oracle $M$, bar $\tau$ and cap $K_{\max}$: increase $S$ from $0$ while $M(2^S)<\tau$ and $2^S\le K_{\max}$; on success return the bracket $(2^{S-1}+1,\,2^S)$ (or $(1,1)$ if $S=0$). Cost $\lceil\log_2K\rceil+1$ oracle calls; guarantee: a ratio-$2$ bracket (Theorem~\ref{thm:geo}). Optional binary refinement inside the bracket returns the exact knee in a further $\lceil\log_2(\text{width})\rceil$ calls, using monotonicity of $M$.

\paragraph{Top-$K$ tokens-per-word prediction.} Given the undiluted knee $K$ and tokenizer counts $w(0),\dots,w(K-1)$ for the top attended words, return the bracket $\bigl(C_w(K-1)+1,\,C_w(K)\bigr)$. Cost $O(K)$; this is the operational form of Theorem~\ref{thm:vardil} and it discriminates the top-$K$ average from the corpus average.

\paragraph{Collision-mass floor.} Given sorted nonnegative weights and bar $\tau$, compute $C=\sum_i p(i)^2$ and return $\lceil\tau^2/C\rceil$ as a lower bound on the knee. Cost $O(n)$; optimal among functions of the collision mass by Theorem~\ref{thm:sharpC}, and scaling by $r$ under dilution by Theorem~\ref{thm:amplify}.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What the measurement did and did not establish}

Honestly stated, the French cell establishes exactly that the knee is at least $33$ at context $1024$ and at least $25$ at context $512$, and, jointly with the earlier cells, that no additive offset of $\pm4$ or even $+8$ describes the shift. Theorem~\ref{thm:under} shows the size of the excess is not merely unmeasured but \emph{unmeasurable} by the instrument used. The correct response is to change the instrument, not to interpolate. The French cell rests on a single corpus source, so its corpus-level generalization is weaker than the four-domain baseline; the theorems above are unconditional statements about attention profiles and are unaffected by that limitation.

\subsection{Why an additive law appeared to hold}

The four earlier domains had tokenization ratios close to one another. Where $r\approx r'$, Theorem~\ref{thm:dilution} gives $K'\approx(r'/r)K$, and over a narrow range of $K$ a multiplicative factor near $1$ is empirically indistinguishable from a small additive offset. The additive rule was a local linearization of a multiplicative law: the offset it predicts is constant, whereas the true shift scales with $rK$.

\subsection{Relation to concentration measures}

The bound $\tau^2/C$ says that the \emph{effective support size} of the attention distribution lower-bounds the budget. Since equality holds for the uniform profile, any refinement must use more than the second moment --- for instance the full R\'enyi spectrum, or the shape of $C_p(k)$ as a function of $k$ rather than a uniform cap. Real attention is peaked with a heavy tail, precisely the regime where such a refinement should be strictly stronger.

\section{Future work}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Top-$K$ tokens-per-word test.} Log the tokenizer's token count for the top-$K$ attended words and compare $C_w(K)$ with the observed knee (Theorem~\ref{thm:vardil}); this discriminates decisively between the corpus-average and top-$K$ predictors.
\item \textbf{Adopt geometric grids.} Replace $\{8,16,24,32\}$ with $\{1,2,4,8,16,32,64\}$: fewer probes reach a higher ceiling and every verdict becomes a two-sided bracket.
\item \textbf{Collision mass as a domain-free predictor.} Measure $C_p$ from attention maps per domain and test whether $\tau^2/C$ tracks the knee ordering; this needs no tokenizer and so extends to modalities where ``word'' is undefined.
\item \textbf{More languages, larger models.} Test whether ratio bands (Corollary~\ref{cor:band}) separate language families as predicted, and whether the multiplicative law persists at scale.
\item \textbf{Beyond the second moment.} Seek two-sided knee estimates from the full profile of $C_p(k)$ or higher R\'enyi masses.
\item \textbf{Mixed-traffic provisioning.} Validate Theorem~\ref{thm:mix} on genuinely mixed multilingual traffic and quantify the cost of max-provisioning against a traffic-weighted average.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

The knee of an attention profile is a multiplicative quantity. It scales with the number of tokens a tokenizer spends on the words a model actually attends to; it is bounded below by $\tau^2/C$, where $C$ caps the collision mass of the attention distribution; and it composes multiplicatively under successive domain shifts. Arithmetic budget grids are therefore the wrong instrument: they can be escaped, and once escaped they certify a lower bound and provably nothing more. Geometric grids cannot be escaped and always return a factor-two bracket at logarithmic cost. Finally, full-context accuracy carries no information about the knee, in the strong sense that both orderings between them are simultaneously realizable. A failed sweep on French prose is, read correctly, not a gap in a table --- it is evidence that the table had the wrong shape.

